local Collider = {}

---
---Reset the internal search structure (the spatial hash). This clears all shapes that were registered beforehand, meaning that HC will not be able to find any collisions with those shapes anymore.
---
---@param cell_size? number The cell size for the spatial hash (default: `100`).
function Collider:resetHash(cell_size) end

---
---Add a rectangle shape to the scene.
---
---**Note:** [`Shape`](lua://HC.Shape) transformations, e.g. [`Shape.moveTo()`](lua://HC.Shape.moveTo) and [`Shape.rotate()`](lua://HC.Shape.rotate) will be with respect to the _center_, not the upper left corner of the rectangle!
---
---@param x number The upper left corner of the rectangle (x-axis).
---@param y number The upper left corner of the rectangle (y-axis).
---@param w number The width of the rectangle.
---@param h number The height of the rectangle.
---@return HC.ConvexPolygonShape -- The shape added to the scene.
function Collider:rectangle(x, y, w, h) end

---
---Add a polygon shape to the scene.
---
---Any non-self-intersecting polygon is valid. The polygon will be closed; the first and last points do not need to be the same.
---
---@param ... number The vertices of the polygon, each as a pair of numbers i.e. `x1, y1, x2, y2...`. At least three corners that do not lie on a straight line are required.
---@return HC.PolygonShape -- The shape added to the scene.
function Collider:polygon(...) end

---
---Add a circle shape to the scene.
---
---@param cx number The center of the circle (x-axis).
---@param cy number The center of the circle (y-axis).
---@param radius number The radius of the circle.
---@return HC.CircleShape -- The shape added to the scene.
function Collider:circle(cx, cy, radius) end

---
---Add a point shape to the scene.
---
---@param x number The position of the point (x-axis).
---@param y number The position of the point (y-axis).
---@return HC.CircleShape -- The shape added to the scene.
function Collider:point(x, y) end

---
---Add a shape to the bookkeeping system.
---
---[`neighbors()`](lua://HC.Collider.neighbors) and [`collisions()`](lua://HC.Collider.collisions) work only with registered shapes. You don’t need to (and should not) register any shapes created with methods on a collider object or the singleton (e.g. [`HC.circle()`](lua://HC.circle), [`collider.rectangle()`](lua://HC.Collider.rectangle)).
---
---**Note:** Overwrites [`Shape.move()`](lua://HC.Shape.move), [`Shape.rotate()`](lua://HC.Shape.rotate), and [`Shape.scale()`](lua://HC.Shape.scale) with versions that update the spatial hash.
---`
---This function is mostly only useful if you provide a [custom shape](https://hc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/Shapes.html#custom-shapes).
---
---@param shape HC.Shape The shape to add to the spatial hash.
function Collider:register(shape) end

---
---Remove a shape from the bookkeeping system. 
---
---**Warning:** This will also invalidate the functions [`Shape.move()`](lua://HC.Shape.move), [`Shape.rotate()`](lua://HC.Shape.rotate), and [`Shape.scale()`](lua://HC.Shape.scale). Make sure you delete the shape from your own actor list(s).
---
---@param shape HC.Shape The shape to remove from the spatial hash.
function Collider:remove(shape) end

---
---Get shapes that are colliding with `shape` and the vectors to separate the shapes.
---
---The separating vector points in the direction that `shape` has to move in order to stop the collision. The length of the vector is the minimal amount that either shape has to move.
---
---The shapes are stored in the keys of the table, and the separating vectors in the corresponding values. You can iterate over the shapes using [`pairs`](lua://pairs) (see [example](https://hc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/MainModule.html#HC.collisions)).
---
---@param shape HC.Shape The shape to query.
---@return {[HC.Shape]: {[0]: number, [1]: number, x: number, y: number}} -- A table that maps colliding shapes to their separating vectors.
function Collider:collisions(shape) end

---
---Get other shapes in the scene that are _close_ to `shape`.
---
---The table is a set, meaning that the shapes are stored in the keys. You can iterate over the shapes using [`pairs`](lua://pairs) (see [example](https://hc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/MainModule.html#HC.neighbors)).
---
---**Note:** The result depends on the size and position of `shape` as well as the grid size of the spatial hash: the method returns the shapes that are in the same cell(s) as shape.
---
---@param shape HC.Shape The shape to query.
---@return {[HC.Shape]: HC.Shape} -- Set containing the shape's neighbors.
function Collider:neighbors(shape) end

---
---Get shapes that contain the point `(x, y)`.
---
---The table is a set, meaning that the shapes are stored in the keys. You can iterate over the shapes using pairs (see [example](https://hc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/MainModule.html#HC.neighbors)).
---
---@return {[HC.Shape]: HC.Shape} -- Set containing the shapes at the point.
function Collider:shapesAt(x, y) end

---
---Get the [`Spatialhash`](lua://HC.Spatialhash) instance.
---
---@return HC.Spatialhash
---@nodiscard
function Collider:hash() end---
